  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 228 AM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows low pressure in place over \nOntario with a trailing cold front across Lake Huron to Central \nIndiana and Western KY. GOES19 shows thunderstorms ahead of the \nfront had advanced to the Ohio river while the rest of the forecast \narea remained clear. Dew points across the area still remained \nrather high, in the middle 60s, and some fog was beginning to form. \nAloft, water vapor shows strong ridging in place over the western \nCONUS, and a deep trough over Ontario and the Great Lakes, resulting \nin a highly amplified flow. Northwest flow and subsidence was found \nacross Indiana.\n\nToday...\n\nModels show the strong ridging over the Rockies continuing to slowly \nbuild eastward. This will keep the northwest flow and lee side \nsubsidence in place, allowing strong surface high pressure to the \nnorthwest to build across Indiana today. Forecast soundings show dry \nair within the mid levels today, but do show signs of afternoon CU \ndevelopment. Thus after morning fog burns off, mostly sunny skies \nwill be expected followed by a partly cloudy afternoon. Look for \nhighs in the lower to middle 80s. \n\nFog development will continue to need to be watched over night and \nshould dense fog become widespread, an advisory may be needed.\n\nTonight through Saturday...\n\nThrough Saturday, the upper ridging to the west continues to slowly \nnudge eastward, keeping Central Indiana under northwest flow aloft \nand ongoing subsidence. Mid level RH is very indicative of this, \nshowing low RH across the region through Saturday. Forecast \nsoundings tonight through Saturday show a very dry column with \nunreachable convective temperatures. Thus mostly sunny days and \nmostly clear nights will be expected. As the associated high \npressure system drifts east of Indiana by Saturday, southerly return \nflow will begin to develop. Thus expect a warming trend in daily \nhigh temperatures, reaching the mid to upper 80s by Saturday.\n\nSunday through Wednesday...\n\nHot weather looks to appear for the end of August and early \nSeptember across Central Indiana. Models are suggesting a strong \narea of upper level high pressure setting up over the deep south, \nwith an associated ridge axis pushing north across the Great Lakes. \nThis looks to keep the main steering flow aloft well north of \nCentral Indiana, keeping any forcing dynamics out of our area. \nStrong surface high pressure looks to set up over the southeastern \nstates extending east to Bermuda. This will result in a steady flow \nof hot and humid air across Central Indiana. Surface temps are \nsuggested to easily reach the 90s each day with dew points surging \ninto the 70s. Early forecast soundings suggest 700mb temps in excess \nof 12C, resulting in a cap with convective temperatures over 100F. \nThus at this point, diurnal storms are not in play, but this could \nchange as things progress and the eventual flow aloft.",
